Z||000000||bHooper||05-29-2001||09:13 PM||||i was going to email you directly, but this is a much better place for the info to be shared!<P>here goes.<P>to swap a clutch into a 6 cyl fiero, it really isnt all that bad (i say this and the jury is still out on my job, even after 1 year and 6000 miles!).<P><BR>1) yes you need to drop the cradle... that is the easiest way.  get a 8' long 4x4.  place it under the car (just forward of the front cradle bolts).  <P>2) obtain 2 pair of jackstands, and preferably 2 jacks, 1 small pallet, 2 2x4's and a set of wheels for moving refigerators.<P>3)with the 4x4 under the car (and a jack under each end), jack the car up enough to remove the rear wheels and to slide your <B><I>Wheeled Pallet</B></I> underneath the cradle.<P>4) with the wheels removed, lower the rear of the car (disconect <B>all</B> hoses, harneses , etc that is attaching the engine to the car.  remove the rear and forward cradle bolts.<P>5) with everything disconected, raise the body of the car again (did i say it may be helpfull to remove the rear bumper?... if not, then this is an excellent time to do so!).  check for any missed conections.  once the rear of the car is high enough to clear the plenum, slide the <B><I>Wheeled Pallet with engine</B></I> out from underneath the car.<P>6) remove the crossover pipe, and seperate the tranny from the block.<P>7) you now have the tranny free so that you can install the new clutch.  when it is time to reinstall the tranny, obtain a pair of 4-6" bolts (with the same thread pattern as the tranny bolts), cut the heads off and screw them into the top holes on the block.  hang the tranny from these, and using a remote starter, kick the engine over until the spline and tranny have mated with the block... bolt her down and repeat the disassembly proceedure to put it back together!<P>good luck!<P>hoop<BR><P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT 5 speed<BR>*Black '86 SE Automatic||||reg||1
